== ARCHIVE: the same three systems in aug-cc-pVDZ, and the reading it kills ==

Companion to `20260930T235900Z_stepm_three_systems_631g.out`. Same three systems, same
method stack, same counterpoise convention, same frozen-core rule; the only change is the
basis, chosen because 6-31g* has no diffuse functions and one of the three dimers is
dispersion-bound.

Run: OMP_NUM_THREADS=16 BASES="aug-cc-pvdz" SYSTEMS="水二聚体,氨二聚体,甲烷二聚体"
     PYTHONPATH=. python3 -u nlcsplit/step_m_ccsdt.py    (PySCF 2.12.1, Python 3.10.12, EXIT=0)
Sizes: water nao=82 (2x41), ammonia nao=100 (2x50), methane nao=118 (2x59); nelec=20,
frozen 2 on the complex and 1+1 on the halves throughout.

    system    basis         MP2     CCSD    CCSD(T)  all-electron   our wB97X-V CP
    water     6-31g*      -7.149   -6.682   -6.856      -6.887          -6.129
    water     aug-cc-pVDZ  -5.210   -4.966   -5.248      -5.327          -6.129
    ammonia   6-31g*      -4.620   -4.293   -4.455      -4.473          -3.675
    ammonia   aug-cc-pVDZ  -3.373   -3.070   -3.331      -3.372          -3.675
    methane   6-31g*      -0.149   -0.129   -0.178      -0.187          -0.466
    methane   aug-cc-pVDZ  -0.918   -0.821   -0.947      -0.981          -0.466

Two readings, and only one of them survives.

KILLED: "the deviation changes sign between systems". On 6-31g* alone the correlated values
sit at -6.856 / -4.455 / -0.178 against functional values of -6.129 / -3.675 / -0.466, i.e.
the functional looks shallower on water and ammonia and deeper on methane. In aug-cc-pVDZ
the methane row moves by a factor of five, -0.178 -> -0.947, and the sign of that comparison
inverts. The 6-31g* methane number was a basis artefact of a dispersion-bound dimer in a
basis without diffuse functions, exactly as the companion archive flagged in advance. The
paper does not claim a sign flip.

SURVIVES: the like-for-like comparison at a single basis, and the reason a cross-basis
verdict is unsupportable. On 6-31g* both sides are computed, and the functional is shallower
by 0.73 (water) and 0.78 (ammonia) kcal/mol and deeper by 0.29 (methane) - a spread of
0.29-0.78 kcal/mol with no single sign. But the same table shows the *correlated* value
moving 0.18 -> 0.95 on methane, 4.46 -> 3.33 on ammonia and 6.86 -> 5.25 on water when the
basis alone changes. A discrepancy between one small-basis density-functional number and one
correlated number therefore cannot separate the functional from the basis, which is the only
thing section 3.2 says about the binding energy.

Also worth recording: the water column reproduces the archived single-system ladder exactly
in both bases (-6.856 / -5.248), so extending the script to three systems moved no number,
and the summary table now refuses to print the 6-31g*-referenced paper row next to a
different basis.

